WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.863
Welcome to the lesson! The following simple
linear

2
00:00:04.863 --> 00:00:07.500
operations can be applied to vectors:

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:13.590
addition, subtraction and multiplication of a
vector by a number. These operations

4
00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.571
are directly related to the corresponding
operations

5
00:00:17.571 --> 00:00:20.480
on vector quantities in mechanics. All

6
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.500
these operations have a clear geometric
meaning.

7
00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:32.140
Let us consider the first operation, the sum
of vectors. Two nonzero vectors a and

8
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:35.080
b are given, and we need to find their sum.

9
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:42.070
Let us find the sum of the vectors. The vector
joining the start of vector a

10
00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.489
to the end of vector b is called the sum of
the

11
00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:48.690
two vectors. Then the sum of vectors a and b

12
00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.390
is the vector c.

13
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:59.040
This way of finding the sum of vectors a and b
is also called the triangle rule.

14
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.578
Let us place the starts of vectors a and b at
a

15
00:01:03.578 --> 00:01:06.740
common point and build on them, as on sides,

16
00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:09.774
a parallelogram. Then the diagonal of the
parallelogram

17
00:01:09.774 --> 00:01:11.540
coming out of this common point,

18
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:18.100
c = a + b, is the sum of the two vectors.

19
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.980
This rule is called the parallelogram rule.

20
00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:31.660
Now let us find the difference of vectors. The
difference of two vectors a and b

21
00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:38.360
is the vector c equal to the sum of vector a
and the vector opposite to b.

22
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:45.340
Let us show it in a drawing: if vectors a and
b come out of one common point,

23
00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:52.020
then the difference of the vectors is the
vector c joining the end of vector b

24
00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.460
to the end of vector a.

25
00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.036
Next, let us consider multiplying a vector by
a number.

26
00:02:00.036 --> 00:02:02.980
When a vector is multiplied by a number λ,

27
00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:06.587
the length of the vector is multiplied by the
absolute

28
00:02:06.587 --> 00:02:09.660
value of λ. Here, when λ is greater than zero,

29
00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.468
the direction of the vector is kept,

30
00:02:12.468 --> 00:02:16.290
and when λ is less than zero it becomes
opposite.

31
00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:23.200
In other words, when multiplied by a number, a
vector is stretched,

32
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.540
when divided by a number it shrinks, and when
multiplied by a negative number

33
00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.880
it changes direction.

34
00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:34.013
Note that all these vectors are collinear:

35
00:02:34.013 --> 00:02:37.520
here one vector is expressed through another.

36
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:45.200
Thus, multiplying a vector by a number gives a
collinear vector.

37
00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:49.649
To sum up: in this lesson we learned to
perform

38
00:02:49.649 --> 00:02:52.960
the following simple operations on vectors:

39
00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:58.040
adding and subtracting vectors, and
multiplying a vector by a number.
